{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01KUYs3HTu6hsYvxo8uYKiSV","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\\documentclass[11pt]{amsart}\n     2→\\usepackage{amsmath,amsthm,amssymb,bm}\n     3→\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4→\\usepackage{indentfirst}\n     5→\\usepackage{graphicx}\n     6→\\usepackage{tikz}\n     7→\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing, decorations.pathmorphing, decorations.shapes}\n     8→\\usetikzlibrary{arrows,calc}\n     9→\\usepackage{mathrsfs,esint}\n    10→\\usepackage{comment}\n    11→\\usepackage{enumerate}\n    12→\\usepackage{hyperref}\n    13→\\hypersetup{\n    14→    colorlinks = true,\n    15→    linkcolor = red,\n    16→    anchorcolor = green!50!black,\n    17→    citecolor = green!50!black,\n    18→    filecolor = green!50!black,\n    19→    urlcolor = green!50!black\n    20→}\n    21→\\usepackage{cleveref}\n    22→\n    23→\\usepackage[top=1in, bottom=1in, left=1.25in, right=1.25in]{geometry}\n    24→\\usepackage{setspace}\n    25→\n    26→\\usepackage{float}\n    27→\n    28→\\usepackage{subcaption}\n    29→\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n    30→\\pgfplotsset{compat=newest, compat/show suggested version=false}\n    31→\\usepackage{tikz}\n    32→\\usetikzlibrary{intersections}\n    33→\n    34→\\usepackage{diagbox}\n    35→\\usepackage{algorithm, algorithmicx, algpseudocode}\n    36→\\numberwithin{algorithm}{section}\n    37→\\usepackage{tabularx}\n    38→\\usepackage{tcolorbox}\n    39→\n    40→\\usepackage[titletoc,toc,title]{appendix}\n    41→\\renewcommand\\appendixname{Appendix}\n    42→\n    43→\\newtheorem{thm}{Theorem}[section]\n    44→\\newtheorem{theorem}[thm]{Theorem}\n    45→\\newtheorem{proposition}[thm]{Proposition}\n    46→\\newtheorem{corollary}[thm]{Corollary}\n    47→\\newtheorem{lemma}[thm]{Lemma}\n    48→\\newtheorem{remark}[thm]{\\textit{Remark}}\n    49→\\newtheorem{definition}[thm]{Definition}\n    50→\\newtheorem{assumption}[thm]{Assumption}\n    51→\\newtheorem{example}[thm]{Example}\n    52→\n    53→\\newcommand{\\titleShort}{Wave PINNs}\n    54→\\newcommand{\\titleLong}{Precise Goal and Verification Conditions for the Weight Construction}\n    55→\n    56→\\title[\\titleShort]{\\titleLong}\n    57→\n    58→\\thanks{}\n    59→\n    60→\\author{Author}\n    61→% \\address{Address}\n    62→% \\email{Email}\n    63→\n    64→\\date{}\n    65→\n    66→% \\renewcommand \\theequation {\\arabic{section}.\\arabic{equation}}\n    67→\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n    68→\n    69→\\begin{document}\n    70→% \\maketitle\n    71→\n    72→%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n    73→\\section{Precise Goal}\n    74→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\n    75→For given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n    76→\\begin{equation*}\n    77→    \\left\\{\n    78→    \\begin{aligned}\n    79→        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n    80→        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n    81→        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n    82→        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    83→    \\end{aligned}\n    84→    \\right.\n    85→\\end{equation*}\n    86→such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n    87→\\begin{equation*}\n    88→    \\left\\{\n    89→    \\begin{aligned}\n    90→        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n    91→        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n    92→        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    93→    \\end{aligned}\n    94→    \\right.\n    95→\\end{equation*}\n    96→\\end{tcolorbox}\n    97→\n    98→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\n    99→The final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n   100→\\begin{equation*}\n   101→    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n   102→    \\geq\n   103→    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n   104→\\end{equation*}\n   105→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   106→\n   107→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n   108→\\begin{enumerate}\n   109→    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n   110→    \\begin{equation*}\n   111→        \\varphi\n   112→        =\n   113→        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n   114→    \\end{equation*}\n   115→\n   116→    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   117→    \\begin{equation*}\n   118→        \\square u\n   119→        =\n   120→        \\partial_{t}^{2} u - c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} u.\n   121→    \\end{equation*}\n   122→\n   123→    \\item $\\widetilde{\\square}$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   124→    \\begin{equation*}\n   125→        \\widetilde{\\square} u\n   126→        =\n   127→        |\\partial_{t} u|^{2} - c^{2}|\\partial_{x} u|^{2}.\n   128→    \\end{equation*}\n   129→\n   130→    \\item $L$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   131→    \\begin{equation*}\n   132→        L \\psi\n   133→        =\n   134→        \\lambda^{2} \\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)\n   135→        -\n   136→        (\\alpha - 1) \\lambda \\varphi (\\square \\psi).\n   137→    \\end{equation*}\n   138→\n   139→    \\item $L_{1}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   140→    \\begin{equation*}\n   141→        \\begin{aligned}\n   142→            L_{1} \\psi\n   143→            &=\n   144→            c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\varphi\n   145→            +\n   146→            \\partial_{t}^{2} \\varphi\\\\\n   147→            &=\n   148→            \\lambda^{2} \\varphi\n   149→            \\left (\n   150→                (\\partial_{t} \\psi)^{2}\n   151→                +\n   152→                c^{2} (\\partial_{x} \\psi)^{2}\n   153→            \\right )\n   154→            +\n   155→            \\lambda \\varphi\n   156→            \\left (\n   157→                \\partial_{t}^{2} \\psi\n   158→                +\n   159→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\psi\n   160→            \\right ).\n   161→        \\end{aligned}\n   162→    \\end{equation*}\n   163→\n   164→    \\item $L_{2}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   165→    \\begin{equation*}\n   166→    \\begin{aligned}\n   167→        L_{2}\n   168→        &=\n   169→        \\frac{1}{2}\n   170→        \\Big (\n   171→            (\\alpha - 1) s \\lambda (\\square (\\varphi (\\square \\psi)))\n   172→            -\n   173→            s \\lambda^{2} (\\square (\\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)))\n   174→        \\Big )\\\\\n   175→        &+\n   176→        \\Big (\n   177→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3} (\\alpha - 1) \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\square \\psi)\n   178→            -\n   179→            s^{3} \\lambda^{4} \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)^{2}\\\\\n   180→            &\\qquad+\n   181→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3}\n   182→            \\left (\n   183→                \\partial_{t} (\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{t} \\psi))\n   184→                +\n   185→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}(\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{x} \\psi))\n   186→            \\right )\n   187→        \\Big ).\n   188→    \\end{aligned}\n   189→    \\end{equation*}\n   190→\\end{enumerate}\n   191→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   192→Note that $\\partial_{x}^{2} u = \\partial_{x} (\\partial_{x} u) = u_{x x}$.\n   193→This problem comes from the Carleman estimate for the wave operator $\\square$.\n   194→$\\psi, s, \\alpha, \\lambda$ could be dependent on $c$ and $T$.\n   195→If multiple $\\psi$ are possible, find the smoothest one.\n   196→\n   197→\\end{document}\n   198→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"760dc304-8a9b-499d-91d9-5ac15b9d654b","uuid":"5fe20aa1-ed2c-4486-8893-a2e6e8b488d0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/problem.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{amsart}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amsthm,amssymb,bm}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{indentfirst}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing, decorations.pathmorphing, decorations.shapes}\n\\usetikzlibrary{arrows,calc}\n\\usepackage{mathrsfs,esint}\n\\usepackage{comment}\n\\usepackage{enumerate}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\hypersetup{\n    colorlinks = true,\n    linkcolor = red,\n    anchorcolor = green!50!black,\n    citecolor = green!50!black,\n    filecolor = green!50!black,\n    urlcolor = green!50!black\n}\n\\usepackage{cleveref}\n\n\\usepackage[top=1in, bottom=1in, left=1.25in, right=1.25in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{setspace}\n\n\\usepackage{float}\n\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n\\pgfplotsset{compat=newest, compat/show suggested version=false}\n\\usepackage{tikz}\n\\usetikzlibrary{intersections}\n\n\\usepackage{diagbox}\n\\usepackage{algorithm, algorithmicx, algpseudocode}\n\\numberwithin{algorithm}{section}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{tcolorbox}\n\n\\usepackage[titletoc,toc,title]{appendix}\n\\renewcommand\\appendixname{Appendix}\n\n\\newtheorem{thm}{Theorem}[section]\n\\newtheorem{theorem}[thm]{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{proposition}[thm]{Proposition}\n\\newtheorem{corollary}[thm]{Corollary}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}[thm]{Lemma}\n\\newtheorem{remark}[thm]{\\textit{Remark}}\n\\newtheorem{definition}[thm]{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{assumption}[thm]{Assumption}\n\\newtheorem{example}[thm]{Example}\n\n\\newcommand{\\titleShort}{Wave PINNs}\n\\newcommand{\\titleLong}{Precise Goal and Verification Conditions for the Weight Construction}\n\n\\title[\\titleShort]{\\titleLong}\n\n\\thanks{}\n\n\\author{Author}\n% \\address{Address}\n% \\email{Email}\n\n\\date{}\n\n% \\renewcommand \\theequation {\\arabic{section}.\\arabic{equation}}\n\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n\n\\begin{document}\n% \\maketitle\n\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n\\section{Precise Goal}\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\nFor given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\nsuch that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\nThe final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n    \\geq\n    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\varphi\n        =\n        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\square u\n        =\n        \\partial_{t}^{2} u - c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} u.\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $\\widetilde{\\square}$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\widetilde{\\square} u\n        =\n        |\\partial_{t} u|^{2} - c^{2}|\\partial_{x} u|^{2}.\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        L \\psi\n        =\n        \\lambda^{2} \\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)\n        -\n        (\\alpha - 1) \\lambda \\varphi (\\square \\psi).\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L_{1}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            L_{1} \\psi\n            &=\n            c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\varphi\n            +\n            \\partial_{t}^{2} \\varphi\\\\\n            &=\n            \\lambda^{2} \\varphi\n            \\left (\n                (\\partial_{t} \\psi)^{2}\n                +\n                c^{2} (\\partial_{x} \\psi)^{2}\n            \\right )\n            +\n            \\lambda \\varphi\n            \\left (\n                \\partial_{t}^{2} \\psi\n                +\n                c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\psi\n            \\right ).\n        \\end{aligned}\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $L_{2}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        L_{2}\n        &=\n        \\frac{1}{2}\n        \\Big (\n            (\\alpha - 1) s \\lambda (\\square (\\varphi (\\square \\psi)))\n            -\n            s \\lambda^{2} (\\square (\\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)))\n        \\Big )\\\\\n        &+\n        \\Big (\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{3} (\\alpha - 1) \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\square \\psi)\n            -\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{4} \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)^{2}\\\\\n            &\\qquad+\n            s^{3} \\lambda^{3}\n            \\left (\n                \\partial_{t} (\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{t} \\psi))\n                +\n                c^{2} \\partial_{x}(\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{x} \\psi))\n            \\right )\n        \\Big ).\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\end{equation*}\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\nNote that $\\partial_{x}^{2} u = \\partial_{x} (\\partial_{x} u) = u_{x x}$.\nThis problem comes from the Carleman estimate for the wave operator $\\square$.\n$\\psi, s, \\alpha, \\lambda$ could be dependent on $c$ and $T$.\nIf multiple $\\psi$ are possible, find the smoothest one.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":198,"startLine":1,"totalLines":198}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Kz27DtgfSrzJDG6zs7GGmk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Failed Approaches Log\n     2→\n     3→This file accumulates all previously tried candidates and why they failed.\n     4→The search agent should read this to avoid repeating dead ends.\n     5→\n     6→---\n     7→\n     8→\n     9→## Round 1\n    10→\n    11→### Candidate\n    12→```python\n    13→# Description: Log-sqrt ansatz: psi = -ln(1+t) - sqrt(x+1), small lambda\n    14→#\n    15→# Key properties:\n    16→# - psi_t = -1/(1+t) -> 0 (bounded derivatives in t)\n    17→# - psi_tt = 1/(1+t)^2 > 0 (convex in t)\n    18→# - psi_x = -1/(2*sqrt(x+1)) -> 0 (derivatives decay in x)\n    19→# - psi_xx = 1/(4*(x+1)^(3/2)) (concave-up correction)\n    20→#\n    21→# With alpha=1:\n    22→# - Condition 1b is EXACTLY: (-L1-L)/phi = c^2/(8*(x+1)^(3/2)) > 0 always\n    23→# - Condition 1a: (L-L1)/phi > 0 verified numerically (holds for t << 1/|lam|^(3/2))\n    24→# - Condition 2: lam*psi = |lam|*(ln(1+t)+sqrt(x+1)) -> +inf as x->inf\n    25→\n    26→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.sqrt(x + 1)\n    27→\n    28→subs_dict = {\n    29→    alpha : 1,\n    30→    s     : -1,\n    31→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 10),\n    32→}\n    33→```\n    34→\n    35→### Why it failed\n    36→## Failure Analysis\n    37→\n    38→### Root Cause: Unbounded growth of (1+t)² terms relative to spatial terms\n    39→\n    40→All three failing conditions share the same structural weakness: the candidate ψ = -log(1+t) - √(x+1) produces operators where certain terms grow as powers of (1+t)² or (1+t)⁴ in the numerator, while the compensating positive spatial terms (like (x+1)^(3/2)) are independent of t. For any fixed x > 24, increasing t eventually overwhelms the spatial terms.\n    41→\n    42→**Specifically:**\n    43→1. **Condition 1a** numerator = c²(1+t)²(5 - √(x+1)) + 20(x+1)^(3/2). The first term is O(t²) and negative for x > 24; the second term is O(1) in t. So the condition fails for t > O(1/c).\n    44→\n    45→2. **Condition 1b** works because both terms in its numerator are positive — this is the key asymmetry. The (1+t)² growth in condition 1b is in a *positive* coefficient (5c²(1+t)²), whereas in condition 1a it multiplies a coefficient that changes sign.\n    46→\n    47→3. **Conditions 3 and sufficient** inherit the same problem through the operators L and L₁ whose difference involves condition-1a-type expressions raised to higher powers.\n    48→\n    49→### Why the engine reported \"unknown\" instead of \"False\"\n    50→\n    51→The verify engine:\n    52→- Uses SymPy's symbolic sign-checking, which returned \"unknown\" because x, t are declared as `real=True` (not `nonnegative=True`), and the expressions involve √(x+1) which SymPy cannot reason about for arbitrary real x.\n    53→- Falls back to numerical sampling on x ∈ [0, 50], t ∈ [0, 10], c ∈ {0.5, 1, 2}. This range is too small to detect failures that occur at t > 28 (for c=2) or t > 57 (for c=1).\n    54→\n    55→### What the next candidate needs\n    56→\n    57→To fix condition 1a, the candidate must ensure that the expression `(L - L₁)/φ` does not have terms that grow with t while being negative for large x. Two strategies:\n    58→\n    59→1. **Make ψ_x decay faster.** The problematic term is `-2c²λ²(ψ_x)²` in `(L-L₁)/φ`. With ψ_x = -1/(2√(x+1)), we get (ψ_x)² = 1/(4(x+1)), which decays only as O(1/x). If ψ_x decayed faster (e.g., exponentially), the negative contribution would be negligible.\n    60→\n    61→2. **Make the \"Laplacian\" ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx grow with t.** Currently ψ_tt = 1/(1+t)² which *decays* with t. If instead ψ_tt grew or stayed constant, it could compensate the growing negative terms. However, ψ_tt > 0 requires concavity which is hard to maintain with growing second derivatives.\n    62→\n    63→3. **Use a separable form ψ(x,t) = f(x) + g(t) where both f and g are chosen so that the cross-terms in the operators maintain consistent signs.** The current failure arises because the x-dependent sign change at x=24 (where √(x+1) = 5 = 1/(2|λ|)) interacts with t-growth. Choosing |λ| even smaller would push this threshold to larger x, but would not eliminate the asymptotic failure.\n    64→\n    65→4. **Consider ψ forms where ψ_x is bounded by a function of t that decays appropriately.** For instance, ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) would give ψ_x = -1/(1+x) with (ψ_x)² = 1/(1+x)² decaying as O(1/x²), and ψ_xx = 1/(1+x)² > 0. But one must check that condition 2 (λψ → +∞) still holds.\n    66→\n    67→5. **Most promising direction:** The fundamental issue is that (ψ_x)² doesn't decay fast enough relative to ψ_xx. For ψ_x ~ -x^(-p), we have (ψ_x)² ~ x^(-2p) and ψ_xx ~ x^(-p-1). The ratio (ψ_x)²/ψ_xx ~ x^(-p+1). For this ratio to → 0 as x → ∞ (so that the λ² gradient term doesn't dominate the λ Laplacian term), we need p > 1. The current candidate has p = 1/2 (from √(x+1)), so p < 1, which is insufficient. **Try p > 1**, e.g., ψ involving -(x+1)^q for q < 1/2, or -log(x+1), which gives p = 1 (borderline).\n    68→\n    69→---\n    70→\n    71→\n    72→## Round 2\n    73→\n    74→### Candidate\n    75→```python\n    76→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz: psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x), lambda=-1/20\n    77→#\n    78→# Key structural properties:\n    79→# - Separated variables: psi = f(t) + g(x) with f=-log(1+t), g=-log(1+x)\n    80→# - psi_t = -1/(1+t), psi_x = -1/(1+x): both decay as O(1/x), O(1/t)\n    81→# - psi_tt = 1/(1+t)^2, psi_xx = 1/(1+x)^2: positive, decaying\n    82→# - Critical: (psi_x)^2 = 1/(1+x)^2 decays as O(1/x^2), same rate as psi_xx\n    83→#   This fixes the Round 1 failure where (psi_x)^2 decayed only as O(1/x)\n    84→#\n    85→# - psi_tt + c^2*psi_xx = 1/(1+t)^2 + c^2/(1+x)^2 = psi_t^2 + c^2*psi_x^2\n    86→#   So the \"Laplacian\" equals the gradient-squared, giving clean operator structure\n    87→#\n    88→# With alpha=1, lam=-1/20:\n    89→# - L1/phi = lam*(lam+1)*P = (-1/20)(19/20)*P = -19/400 * P < 0 always ✓\n    90→# - (L-L1)/phi = (1/20)/(1+t)^2 + (9/200)*c^2/(1+x)^2 > 0 (sum of positives) ✓\n    91→# - (-L1-L)/phi = (9/200)/(1+t)^2 + (1/20)*c^2/(1+x)^2 > 0 (sum of positives) ✓\n    92→# - Condition 2: lam*psi = (1/20)*(log(1+t)+log(1+x)) -> +inf as x->inf ✓\n    93→# - Sufficient: (L1^2-L^2)/phi^2 = product of two sums of positives, dominates c^2*(phi_xt)^2 ✓\n    94→# - L2: numerically verified non-negative for c in [0.5,100], x,t in [0,10000] ✓\n    95→\n    96→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n    97→\n    98→subs_dict = {\n    99→    alpha : 1,\n   100→    s     : -1,\n   101→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n   102→}\n   103→```\n   104→\n   105→### Why it failed\n   106→## Failure Analysis\n   107→\n   108→### Why L₂ ≥ 0 fails\n   109→\n   110→The L₂ operator is the most complex condition, involving products and derivatives of φ³ with various ψ-derivative combinations. For the double-logarithm ansatz ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x) with λ = −1/20, the numerator of L₂ (after extracting the manifestly positive denominator) reduces to:\n   111→\n   112→f(z, R) = (2301 − 112R)·z² + (38 − 4R)·z + (2301 + 116R)\n   113→\n   114→where z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² and R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10).\n   115→\n   116→**Root cause:** The coefficient of z² is (2301 − 112R), which changes sign at R ≈ 20.5. For R > 20.5 (i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > ~1.34×10¹³), the parabola opens downward and f eventually becomes negative for large enough z (large enough c²(1+t)²/(1+x)²).\n   117→\n   118→Structurally, this arises because:\n   119→1. The L₂ operator contains terms like −s·λ⁴·φ³·(ψ̃□ψ)² which grow as the fourth power of the \"gradient energy\" tilde_square(ψ).\n   120→2. For the log ansatz, tilde_square(ψ) = 1/(1+t)² − c²/(1+x)², which does not decay fast enough relative to the positive \"Laplacian\" terms in L₂.\n   121→3. The exponent 1/10 = 2λ in the R factor comes from φ³ containing (TX)^(3/20), and this fractional power grows slower than the polynomial terms in the numerator, eventually failing to dominate.\n   122→\n   123→### What would fix it\n   124→\n   125→To satisfy L₂ ≥ 0 globally, the candidate needs one of:\n   126→\n   127→1. **Smaller |λ|**: Reducing |λ| suppresses the λ⁴ term relative to the λ² and λ³ terms. However, very small |λ| may cause issues with condition 2 (needing λψ → +∞ at a sufficient rate) or may introduce new difficulties. The critical question is whether there exists a λ small enough to make the c⁴ coefficient non-negative for all R. Since the negative term grows as −112R = −112(TX)^(1/10) while the positive term is 2301 (constant), no fixed λ in this ansatz family can prevent the sign flip at large R—the issue is structural.\n   128→\n   129→2. **Faster-decaying ψ in t**: The problematic z² = c⁴(1+t)⁴/(1+x)⁴ term grows with t at fixed x. If ψ_t decayed faster than 1/(1+t) (e.g., ψ_t ~ 1/(1+t)^p with p > 1), then z would grow more slowly and might stay below the critical threshold. However, this must be balanced against the need for ψ_tt/ψ_t² to remain O(1) to maintain condition 1.\n   130→\n   131→3. **Different ansatz structure**: The fundamental issue is that the \"double-log\" separated-variable form ψ = f(t) + g(x) with f, g both logarithmic creates a situation where the L₂ fourth-order terms are not dominated at infinity. A non-separated ansatz (e.g., ψ = −log(1 + t + x)) or an ansatz with coupled (t,x) terms might provide better L₂ behavior by ensuring the mixed-derivative structure of L₂ produces additional positive contributions.\n   132→\n   133→4. **Bounded spatial domain**: If x were restricted to a compact domain (e.g., x ∈ [0, M] for some fixed M), the condition would hold since it passes numerically on bounded grids. But the problem likely requires x ∈ [0, ∞), making this not a valid fix.\n   134→\n   135→### Summary for next round\n   136→The double-log ansatz successfully solves conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition—a major improvement over Round 1. The remaining obstacle is L₂ ≥ 0, which fails due to the c⁴(1+t)⁴ term in L₂ growing faster than the R-dependent positive terms at asymptotically large (1+t)(1+x). The search agent should either:\n   137→- Try |λ| < 1/20 and check if L₂ can be made non-negative (unlikely to help fundamentally since the sign-flip in the z² coefficient is independent of λ magnitude once λ is fixed)\n   138→- Explore non-logarithmic or non-separated ansatz forms where the L₂ operator produces manifestly non-negative expressions\n   139→- Consider power-law ansätze ψ = −(1+t)^a − (1+x)^b with 0 < a,b < 1 chosen so that (ψ_x)²/ψ_xx and the L₂ fourth-order terms remain controlled\n   140→\n   141→---\n   142→\n   143→\n   144→## Round 3\n   145→\n   146→### Candidate\n   147→```python\n   148→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with smaller |lambda|=-1/50 to fix L2\n   149→#\n   150→# This is the SAME functional form as Round 2 (psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x))\n   151→# but with lambda = -1/50 instead of -1/20.\n   152→#\n   153→# Round 2 passed conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition.\n   154→# The ONLY failure was L2 >= 0, which failed because the z^2 coefficient\n   155→# (2301 - 112*R) in the L2 quadratic flipped sign at R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) ~ 20.5.\n   156→#\n   157→# With lambda = -1/50:\n   158→# - R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(2/50) = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/25), which grows much slower\n   159→# - The polynomial coefficients in L2 change: the constant positive term scales as\n   160→#   ~N^4 while the R-dependent negative term scales as ~N^3, making the ratio\n   161→#   ~N, so the critical R_crit grows with N.\n   162→# - For N=50, numerical testing over x,t in [1, 10^14] and c in {0.5, 1, 2, 5}\n   163→#   found L2 strictly positive everywhere.\n   164→#\n   165→# Key properties (identical structure to Round 2):\n   166→# - psi_t = -1/(1+t), psi_x = -1/(1+x)\n   167→# - (psi_t)^2 = psi_tt, (psi_x)^2 = psi_xx  (\"self-similar\" property)\n   168→# - With alpha=1: L1/phi = lam(lam+1)*P where P = (1/(1+t)^2 + c^2/(1+x)^2) > 0\n   169→#   and lam(lam+1) = (-1/50)(49/50) = -49/2500 < 0, so L1 < 0. Condition 1 satisfied.\n   170→# - Condition 2: lam*psi = (1/50)*log((1+t)(1+x)) -> +inf\n   171→# - Sufficient: (L1^2 - L^2)/phi^2 = positive polynomial in 1/(1+t)^2 and c^2/(1+x)^2\n   172→\n   173→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n   174→\n   175→subs_dict = {\n   176→    alpha : 1,\n   177→    s     : -1,\n   178→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 50),\n   179→}\n   180→```\n   181→\n   182→### Why it failed\n   183→## Failure Analysis\n   184→\n   185→### The L₂ ≥ 0 condition is the sole failure — same as Round 2\n   186→\n   187→The candidate passes conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition with clean, rigorous proofs. The only failure is L₂ ≥ 0 (condition 3), which fails when (1+t)(1+x) becomes astronomically large.\n   188→\n   189→### Why the parameter change λ: −1/20 → −1/50 didn't fix it\n   190→\n   191→The search agent's reasoning was correct that making |λ| smaller pushes the failure threshold to larger values:\n   192→- Round 2 (λ = −1/20): R_crit = 14751/292... wait, the coefficients change with N. For N = 20: R_crit ≈ 20.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10¹³·³\n   193→- Round 3 (λ = −1/50): R_crit ≈ 50.5, failure at (1+t)(1+x) > ~10⁴²·⁶\n   194→\n   195→But the failure is **structural to the entire family** λ = −1/N with the double-log ansatz. Here is why:\n   196→\n   197→For λ = −1/N, the L₂ expression (after normalization) becomes a quadratic in z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² with:\n   198→- z² coefficient: `(const_pos)·N⁴ − (const_pos)·N³·R` where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(2/N)\n   199→- The z² coefficient flips sign at R_crit ~ O(N)\n   200→- R_crit ~ N means (1+t)(1+x) ~ N^(N/2), which grows super-exponentially with N\n   201→- BUT: for any finite N, there exist t, x, c values making L₂ < 0\n   202→\n   203→The fix cannot come from making N larger within this family. The search agent should consider:\n   204→\n   205→1. **A fundamentally different ansatz for ψ** — the double-log ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x) has the perfect \"self-similar\" property for conditions 1 and the sufficient condition, but its L₂ structure inherently produces an R-dependent negative term that grows faster than the constant positive term.\n   206→\n   207→2. **Modifying the ansatz to suppress the R-dependent term in L₂** — the term involving φ³ · (tilde_square(ψ))² in L₂ generates the problematic −lam⁴·φ³·(tilde_box(ψ))² contribution. An ansatz where tilde_box(ψ) decays faster relative to the other L₂ terms might avoid this.\n   208→\n   209→3. **Exploring ansätze where L₂ is manifestly non-negative** — for instance, if ψ satisfies □ψ = 0 (wave equation) AND ψ̃ = 0, many of the problematic L₂ terms vanish. But this severely constrains ψ and may conflict with condition 2.\n   210→\n   211→4. **Considering non-separated ansätze** — the candidate log notes that the double-log is \"the unique separated ansatz with the self-similar property.\" Breaking the separability assumption might unlock new possibilities, though it would complicate all the other conditions.\n   212→\n   213→### Key mathematical insight for the next round\n   214→\n   215→The L₂ operator has a term −s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² which is always **negative** (since s = −1, s³ = −1, λ⁴ > 0, φ³ > 0, (ψ̃)² ≥ 0). This term must be dominated by the positive terms in L₂. For the double-log ansatz, this negative term grows as φ³ · (1/(1+t)² − c²/(1+x)²)², which involves φ³ = ((1+t)(1+x))^(3/N). The competing positive terms from the □(φ·□ψ) and □(φ·ψ̃) parts grow more slowly because they involve fewer powers of φ. For any finite N, the φ³ factor eventually wins.\n   216→\n   217→An ansatz that makes ψ̃ ≡ 0 (i.e., |ψ_t| = c|ψ_x|) would kill this problematic term entirely, but would need ψ to satisfy the eikonal equation, which may conflict with condition 2 requirements.\n   218→\n   219→---\n   220→\n   221→\n   222→## Round 4\n   223→\n   224→### Candidate\n   225→```python\n   226→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with small |s| to fix L₂\n   227→#\n   228→# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-3: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n   229→# but with s = -1/100 (instead of s = -1).\n   230→#\n   231→# KEY INSIGHT: The L₂ operator decomposes as:\n   232→#   L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n   233→# where (with α=1):\n   234→#   Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃)     ∝ |s|    (POSITIVE, since □(φψ̃) > 0 for double-log)\n   235→#   Term_D = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²        ∝ |s|³   (POSITIVE)\n   236→#   Term_E = s³λ³·(div terms)     ∝ |s|³   (MIXED sign, contains growing φ³ factor)\n   237→#\n   238→# For |s| << 1, the O(|s|) positive Term_B dominates the O(|s|³) mixed Term_E.\n   239→# The failure scale moves to R_crit ~ O(1/|s|²), which with |s| = 1/100 gives\n   240→# R_crit ~ 10⁴, meaning (1+t)(1+x) ~ 10^(10⁴·N/2), astronomically large.\n   241→#\n   242→# Crucially, on the actual domain [0,∞)×[0,T] with fixed T and c:\n   243→# - When z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 1: R is bounded by (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), so L₂ > 0\n   244→# - When z < 1: the large-R asymptotics give L₂ ~ (29 - 28z² - z)R > 0 since z < 1\n   245→# So L₂ > 0 everywhere on the problem domain for ANY fixed c, T > 0.\n   246→#\n   247→# Conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition are identical to Rounds 2-3\n   248→# (they don't depend on s at all), and were proven true there.\n   249→\n   250→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n   251→\n   252→subs_dict = {\n   253→    alpha : 1,\n   254→    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n   255→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n   256→}\n   257→```\n   258→\n   259→### Why it failed\n   260→## Failure Analysis\n   261→\n   262→### Only failing condition: L₂ ≥ 0 (Condition 3)\n   263→\n   264→**What fails:** The L₂ expression involves a competition between:\n   265→- A \"constant\" part A(z) = 5752500z² + 95000z + 5752500, which is always positive and does not grow with (1+t)(1+x).\n   266→- An \"R-growing\" part B(z)·R where R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} grows without bound.\n   267→\n   268→For z > 1 (which occurs when c(1+t) > (1+x)), the R-coefficient B(z) is positive. The ratio A(z)/B(z) has a minimum of ≈205437 over z > 1, so L₂ becomes negative when R exceeds this threshold.\n   269→\n   270→**Why the small-|s| strategy helps but doesn't fully solve it:** Reducing |s| from 1 (Rounds 2–3) to 1/100 (Round 4) increased the threshold from R_crit ≈ 20 to R_crit ≈ 205437 — a factor of ~10⁴ improvement. This pushed the failure from (1+t)(1+x) > 10¹³ to > 10⁵³. However, for the full problem (all c > 0), any fixed s still leads to failure at large enough c.\n   271→\n   272→**Why the candidate log's argument is incomplete:** The candidate log argues that on [0,∞) × [0,T] with fixed c, T, the region z > 1 has bounded R, so L₂ ≥ 0. This is correct for any **specific** (c,T). However, the problem demands conditions hold for **arbitrary** c > 0, meaning the same ψ and s must work for ALL c simultaneously. With s = −1/100 fixed, choosing c > ~10⁵³ (with T = 1) produces R_max > f_min, making L₂ negative.\n   273→\n   274→### Root cause\n   275→The fundamental issue is that the parameters (s, α, λ) are **constants** (not depending on c), while the L₂ expression has terms that grow with c. Specifically, in the z > 1 regime, R_max(c,T) = (c(1+T)²)^{1/10} grows as c^{1/10}, which eventually exceeds f_min for any fixed s.\n   276→\n   277→### How to fix it (guidance for next round)\n   278→\n   279→**Option A: Make s depend on c.** The problem explicitly allows s to depend on c and T. If s is chosen as s = −1/c^k for appropriate k > 0 (or s = −1/(c^a (1+T)^b)), then f_min scales as 1/|s|² → c^{2k}, while R_max scales as c^{1/10}. Choosing k > 1/20 would make f_min grow faster than R_max, ensuring L₂ ≥ 0 for all c.\n   280→\n   281→**Concrete suggestion:** Keep ψ = −log(1+t) − log(1+x), α = 1, λ = −1/20, but set:\n   282→- s = −1/(c² · (1+T)²)^{1/10}  (or equivalently s depending on c to ensure R_max < f_min)\n   283→\n   284→This would make the L₂ \"constant\" terms scale as 1/s² ∝ c^{2/5} while R_max scales as c^{1/10}, ensuring the constant terms dominate for all c.\n   285→\n   286→**Important caveat:** Verify that making s depend on c does not break Conditions 1, 2, or the Sufficient condition. For this ansatz, L, L₁, φ_xt do NOT depend on s (s only appears in L₂), so Conditions 1, 2, and the Sufficient condition remain unaffected.\n   287→\n   288→**Option B: Change the functional form of ψ.** Find a ψ for which L₂ is manifestly non-negative without requiring the constant-vs-R competition. For example, if the L₂ expression can be written as a sum of squares or as a product of non-negative factors, the condition would hold universally.\n   289→\n   290→---\n   291→\n   292→\n   293→## Round 5\n   294→\n   295→### Candidate\n   296→```python\n   297→# Description: Coupled log ansatz ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(1+x) with small |s|\n   298→#\n   299→# NEW FUNCTIONAL FORM: ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(1+x)\n   300→# This is fundamentally different from the double-log ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n   301→# used in Rounds 2-4. The key difference: the first log argument includes a\n   302→# COUPLING between x and t through the term (x + ct), which is always ≥ 0\n   303→# and travels along a characteristic direction of the wave equation.\n   304→#\n   305→# KEY STRUCTURAL PROPERTIES:\n   306→# - ψ_t = -c/(1+x+ct), ψ_x = -(ct+2x+2)/((1+x)(1+x+ct))\n   307→# - box(ψ) = -c²/(1+x)² (only depends on x! MUCH simpler than double-log)\n   308→# - tilde_square(ψ) = c²((1+x)² - (ct+2x+2)²)/((1+x)²(1+x+ct)²) < 0 always\n   309→# - ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx = 2c²/(1+x+ct)² + c²/(1+x)² > 0 always\n   310→#\n   311→# CONDITION 1 (alpha=1, lam=-1/20):\n   312→# The numerators of (L-L₁)/φ and (-L₁-L)/φ are:\n   313→#   1a: 9c²(ct)² + 16c(ct)(1+x) + 16c(ct) + 26(1+x)² - positive definite for N>3\n   314→#   1b: 10c²(ct)² + 20c(ct)(1+x) + 20c(ct) + 29(1+x)² - positive definite\n   315→# Both are manifestly positive sums → condition 1 TRUE.\n   316→#\n   317→# CONDITION 2: λψ = (1/20)log((1+x+ct)(1+x)) → +∞ as x→∞ ✓\n   318→#\n   319→# SUFFICIENT CONDITION: The polynomial numerator has all positive coefficients\n   320→# as a function of ct and (1+x), so it's manifestly positive. ✓\n   321→#\n   322→# CONDITION 3 (L₂ ≥ 0): With |s| = 1/100, the L₂ condition holds on\n   323→# the engine's numerical grid. The O(|s|) positive term dominates the\n   324→# O(|s|³) R-growing term for all (x,t,c) in any bounded region.\n   325→# The coupling (x+ct) structure may help control L₂ growth compared\n   326→# to the separated double-log, since the characteristic direction\n   327→# aligns the phi growth with the tilde_square decay.\n   328→\n   329→psi = -sp.ln(1 + x + c*t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n   330→\n   331→subs_dict = {\n   332→    alpha : 1,\n   333→    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n   334→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n   335→}\n   336→```\n   337→\n   338→### Why it failed\n   339→## Failure Analysis\n   340→\n   341→### Only failing condition: L₂ ≥ 0 (Condition 3)\n   342→\n   343→**What fails:** The L₂ expression has the same structural competition as in Rounds 2–4:\n   344→- N = B(u,v)·R − A(u,v), where R = ((1+x)(1+x+ct))^{1/10} grows without bound as x → ∞\n   345→- B and A are both homogeneous degree-3 polynomials with positive coefficients\n   346→- The ratio A/B is bounded (between ~63411 and ~205446), so R eventually exceeds it\n   347→\n   348→**Comparison with Round 4 (separated double-log):**\n   349→\n   350→| Aspect | Round 4 (separated log) | Round 5 (coupled log) |\n   351→|--------|------------------------|----------------------|\n   352→| L₂ failure threshold | R > ~205437, requires c > ~10⁵³ | R > ~63411, requires x > ~10²⁴ |\n   353→| Failure regime | Large c (with fixed s, t) | Large x (at t = 0, any c) |\n   354→| Minimum of A/B | ~205437 (at z* ≈ 212) | ~63411 (at z = 0) |\n   355→| A/B monotonicity | Has interior minimum | Strictly increasing |\n   356→\n   357→**Round 5 is actually worse than Round 4** in terms of the L₂ failure threshold:\n   358→- Round 4: A/B had a minimum of ~205437 (reached only in the z > 1 regime, requiring large c)\n   359→- Round 5: A/B has its minimum at z = 0 with value ~63411, meaning the failure occurs at large x with t = 0 for any c > 0\n   360→- The failure threshold dropped from ~10⁵³ to ~10²⁴\n   361→\n   362→**Why the coupling didn't help:** The candidate log hypothesized that coupling x and t through the characteristic direction would give better L₂ structure. However, the L₂ expression still decomposes into a bounded ratio A/B competing against a growing R = φ³ factor. The coupling changed the polynomial coefficients but did not eliminate the structural mismatch. In fact, the asymmetry between the (1+x) and (1+x+ct) factors in the coupled form created a less favorable ratio at z = 0 (the t = 0 boundary), where the separated form had a larger margin.\n   363→\n   364→**Root cause (unchanged from Round 4):** The fundamental issue remains:\n   365→1. The L₂ expression contains terms proportional to φ³ = ((1+x)(1+x+ct))^{3/20} that grow without bound\n   366→2. The \"constant\" terms (from the O(|s|) part of L₂) cannot keep up with this growth\n   367→3. The ratio A/B is bounded by the polynomial coefficients, while R = φ^{3/...} is unbounded\n   368→4. Any fixed s (not depending on x, t, c) leads to this competition\n   369→\n   370→### Guidance for next round\n   371→\n   372→**What has been tried and failed (Rounds 2–5):**\n   373→- ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x): L₂ fails at large c (threshold ~10⁵³ with s = -1/100)\n   374→- ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(1+x): L₂ fails at large x (threshold ~10²⁴ with s = -1/100)\n   375→- Reducing |s| helps push the threshold higher but cannot eliminate the structural issue\n   376→\n   377→**The core structural barrier:** For any log-based ψ with constant parameters, the L₂ expression has the form N = B·R − A where R grows as a power of (spatial extent) and A/B is bounded. This barrier appears to be a property of the **entire log-based ansatz family**, not just specific parameter choices.\n   378→\n   379→**Option A: Make s depend on c (and possibly T).** The problem allows s = s(c, T). Since L, L₁, φ_xt do NOT depend on s, Conditions 1, 2, and the Sufficient condition are unaffected. For ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) (Round 4 form), setting s ∝ −1/c^k with k > 1/20 would make the \"constant\" A terms scale as c^{2k} while R_max (over the domain [0,∞) × [0,T]) scales as c^{1/10}, ensuring A dominates B·R for all c. **However**, note that for the Round 5 form, the failure occurs at large x (not large c), so s depending on c alone does NOT fix this form.\n   380→\n   381→**Option B: Return to ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) with s = s(c, T).** This is the most promising path:\n   382→- Conditions 1, 2, Sufficient are already proven (Round 4)\n   383→- The only failure is L₂ at large c\n   384→- Setting s = −1/(c^a · (1+T)^b) for appropriate a, b controls the c-growth\n   385→- Concrete suggestion: s = −c^{-1/5} should work (makes f_min ~ c^{2/5} vs R_max ~ c^{1/10})\n   386→\n   387→**Option C: Fundamentally different function class.** If s must remain constant, one would need a ψ where L₂ is manifestly non-negative (e.g., a sum of squares). This appears structurally impossible for log-based forms but might be achievable with exponential decay (ψ = −e^{−ax} − e^{−bt}), power-law (ψ = −(1+x)^{−p} − (1+t)^{−q}), or other function classes. However, these would need to be checked against all four conditions simultaneously.\n   388→\n   389→**Option D: Verify whether the verify engine supports s = s(c, T).** The candidate file defines `subs_dict` with constant values. If the engine allows s to be a symbolic expression in c (and T, if T is a symbol), Option B becomes immediately testable.\n   390→\n   391→---\n   392→\n   393→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still