→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    28→- Status: **True** (proven)\n    29→- λψ = (1/20)·log(1+x) + (1/20)·log(1+x+ct)\n    30→- Limit value: +∞\n    31→- Diagnosis: As x → +∞, both log(1+x) → +∞ and log(1+x+ct) → +∞, so λψ = (1/20)·log((1+x)(1+x+ct)) → +∞. This is immediate. ∎\n    32→\n    33→### Necessary Condition 3: L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    34→- Status: **False** (L₂ < 0 for sufficiently large x, even at t = 0)\n    35→- Symbolic expression: L₂ = −c⁴ · N / (80000000000 · (1+x)^{79/20} · (1+x+ct)^{59/20}), where the numerator N factors as:\n    36→\n    37→  N = B(u, v) · R − A(u, v)\n    38→\n    39→  with u = ct, v = 1+x, R = ((1+x)(1+x+ct))^{1/10} = (v(u+v))^{1/10}, and:\n    40→  - B(u, v) = 28u³ + 136u²v + 237uv² + 203v³ (all positive coefficients)\n    41→  - A(u, v) = 5752500u³ + 20767500u²v + 27887500uv² + 12872500v³ (all positive coefficients, factored: 2500(u+v)(2301u² + 6006uv + 5149v²))\n    42→\n    43→  Since the prefactor is −c⁴/(positive) < 0, we have L₂ ≥ 0 iff N ≤ 0, i.e., **B · R ≤ A**.\n    44→\n    45→- Numerical sampling (engine's grid x ∈ [0,50], t ∈ [0,10]): min = 2.05×10⁻¹² > 0 → \"likely_true\" on the sampled grid.\n    46→\n    47→- **Detailed analysis and proof of failure:**\n    48→\n    49→  Both B and A are homogeneous degree-3 polynomials in (u, v) with all positive coefficients, so B, A > 0 for u ≥ 0, v > 0. Setting z = u/v:\n    50→  - A/B = C_poly(z)/R_poly(z) where C_poly(z) = 5752500z³ + 20767500z² + 27887500z + 12872500 and R_poly(z) = 28z³ + 136z² + 237z + 203\n    51→  - The derivative d(A/B)/dz has numerator 5000·(40170z⁴ + 232997z³ + 710236z² + 986057z + 522076), which has **all positive coefficients** → strictly positive for z ≥ 0.\n    52→  - Therefore **ratio(z) = A/B is strictly increasing** on [0, ∞).\n    53→  - ratio(0) = 12872500/203 ≈ 63411.33\n    54→  - ratio(z) → 5752500/28 ≈ 205446.43 as z → ∞\n    55→\n    56→  The condition B·R ≤ A is equivalent to R ≤ ratio(z). Since R = v^{1/5} · (1+z)^{1/10} and ratio(z) is bounded above by ~205446, for any fixed z the condition fails when:\n    57→\n    58→  **v^{1/5} · (1+z)^{1/10} > ratio(z)**\n    59→\n    60→  At **z = 0** (i.e., t = 0 or c = 0, but c > 0 is required so t = 0): the condition becomes v^{1/5} > 63411.33, which fails when:\n    61→\n    62→  **v > (63411.33)⁵ ≈ 1.03 × 10²⁴**, i.e., **x > ~1.03 × 10²⁴**\n    63→\n    64→  **Concrete counterexample:** At t = 0, c = 1, x = 2 × 10²⁴:\n    65→  - N = B·R − A ≈ +1.47 × 10⁷⁹ > 0\n    66→  - L₂ ≈ −3.87 × 10⁻¹⁰⁰ < 0\n    67→\n    68→  Verification at x = 10²³ (below threshold): N ≈ −4.79 × 10⁷⁵ < 0, so L₂ > 0 ✓\n    69→\n    70→  Since the problem requires L₂ ≥ 0 for **all** x ≥ 0, t ≥ 0, c > 0, this candidate **fails** Condition 3.\n    71→\n    72→### Sufficient Condition: (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)²\n    73→- Status: **True** (proven analytically; engine reports \"unknown\" due to SymPy limitations)\n    74→- Symbolic expression: c⁴ · Q(u, w) / (160000 · (1+x)^{39/10} · (1+x+ct)^{39/10}), where u = ct, w = 1+x, and:\n    75→\n    76→  Q(u, w) = 360u⁴ + 1360u³w + 3363u²w² + 3972uw³ + 2692w⁴\n    77→\n    78→- Numerical sampling: min = 2.74×10⁻¹⁰ > 0 (consistent with proof).\n    79→- Diagnosis:\n    80→\n    81→  **Proof:** The denominator is always positive. The numerator is c⁴ · Q(u, w) with all coefficients of Q being **strictly positive**: 360, 1360, 3363, 3972, 2692. Since u = ct ≥ 0 and w = 1+x ≥ 1 > 0, every monomial u^a · w^b is non-negative, and the w⁴ term equals 2692·w⁴ > 0. Therefore Q > 0 for all x ≥ 0, t ≥ 0, c > 0. ∎\n    82→\n    83→  **Additional verification:** Treating Q as a polynomial in u with w = 1, the quartic Q(u, 1) = 360u⁴ + 1360u³ + 3363u² + 3972u + 2692 has all four roots with negative real parts (≈ −0.977 ± 1.002i and −0.912 ± 1.729i), confirming Q(u, 1) > 0 for all u ≥ 0.\n    84→\n    85→## Overall Summary\n    86→- Necessary condition 1: **True** (proven: positive-definite quadratic forms with negative discriminants)\n    87→- Necessary condition 2: **True** (proven: