{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01V1TvKUSu4NqBBXZPrsp4G1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"L2 is going negative at large t! The failure mode is at large t, not large x. This makes sense — when t is large and x is small, the z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² term is large, and R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} grows with t. With s depending on c, the c-growth is controlled, but the t-growth isn't.\n\nThe problem says s may depend on c **and T**. If we use s = s(c, T), we can control the t-growth since t ∈ [0, T]. Let me check if T is available as a symbol."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1015,"cache_read_input_tokens":136532,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1015,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"760dc304-8a9b-499d-91d9-5ac15b9d654b","uuid":"bec9eba7-0e5c-4a29-8839-636eda38033a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01V1TvKUSu4NqBBXZPrsp4G1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Ak3rm64FkAgGFap91x3pvb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"source /home/cyanz/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate wave && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN')\n\n# Check if T is available. The problem says s can depend on c and T.\n# The verify engine injects: x, t, c, x0, alpha, s, lam, sp\n# T is NOT injected. But we can define it in the candidate file.\n# Actually, the prompt says \"subs_dict values may depend on c\"\n# Let's check if we can use a T symbol.\n\n# The numerical check samples t in [0, 10]. But the problem requires [0, T] for arbitrary T.\n# The failure occurs at large t. \n# \n# KEY ISSUE: the problem domain is [0, inf) x [0, T] for GIVEN T.\n# So s = s(c, T) is allowed - it's a constant for each (c, T) pair.\n#\n# However, the verify engine doesn't have T as a symbol. It tests over a grid t ∈ [0, 10].\n# We need to ensure L2 ≥ 0 for ALL t ∈ [0, T] and x ∈ [0, ∞).\n#\n# The R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} grows as:\n#  - x^{1/10} for fixed t (i.e., R unbounded as x -> inf for any fixed t)\n#  - t^{1/10} for fixed x\n#\n# So for the separated double-log with ANY constant s, R is unbounded\n# as x -> infinity, and L2 becomes negative.\n# Even with s = s(c, T), R grows as x -> infinity for any fixed T.\n#\n# This means the double-log ansatz has a FUNDAMENTAL problem with L2:\n# regardless of s, the L2 condition fails at sufficiently large x.\n#\n# Wait... let me re-examine. The failure analysis for Round 4 said:\n# \"the failure was for ALL c > 0, meaning the same ψ and s must work for ALL c simultaneously\"\n# But for the x -> infinity failure, even a single c is enough.\n#\n# So the double-log ansatz CANNOT satisfy L2 > 0 on [0, ∞) × [0, T]\n# for any choice of parameters (as long as the L2 structure has the form\n# N = B·R - A with bounded A/B ratio and unbounded R).\n#\n# This means I need a FUNDAMENTALLY DIFFERENT ansatz.\n# The log-based family is structurally unable to satisfy L2.\n\nprint(\"The double-log ansatz cannot satisfy L2 > 0 on [0,inf) x [0,T]\")\nprint(\"regardless of parameter choices including s(c,T).\")\nprint(\"Reason: R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{1/10} grows unboundedly as x -> infinity,\")\nprint(\"while the ratio A/B is bounded.\")\nprint()\nprint(\"Need a fundamentally different ansatz family.\")\nprint()\n\n# Let me think about what properties ψ needs:\n# 1. Conditions 1, 2, Sufficient must hold\n# 2. L2 > 0 must hold\n# \n# The L2 has a problematic term: -s^3 * lam^4 * phi^3 * (tilde_sq(psi))^2\n# Since s^3 < 0, lam^4 > 0, phi^3 > 0, (tilde)^2 >= 0, this whole term is NON-NEGATIVE.\n# Wait: -s^3 * lam^4 * phi^3 * (tilde)^2 = -(-|s|)^3 * lam^4 * phi^3 * (tilde)^2\n# = |s|^3 * lam^4 * phi^3 * (tilde)^2 >= 0\n# So this term is actually POSITIVE!\n# \n# The problematic terms in L2 must come from the divergence terms (Term_C).\n# Or from the sq(phi*tilde) part having wrong sign.\n# But we showed sq(phi*tilde) > 0 for the double-log.\n# And -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde) = (1/2)|s|*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde) > 0.\n# So Term_A > 0 and Term_B >= 0.\n#\n# The issue must be in Term_C: s^3*lam^3*(div terms) = -|s|^3*(-1/N)^3*(div terms)\n# = |s|^3/N^3 * (div terms)\n# If div terms are negative, this gives a negative contribution.\n#\n# For the double-log: the divergence terms involve derivatives of phi^3*tilde*grad(psi).\n# These can be negative.\n#\n# ALTERNATIVE APPROACH: What if tilde_sq(psi) = 0 everywhere?\n# Then ALL the s^3 terms vanish, and L2 = Term_A only.\n# Term_A = -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*0) = 0. So L2 = 0. Not helpful.\n#\n# What if tilde_sq(psi) = constant? Or has a specific sign?\n#\n# Actually, let me reconsider: maybe a QUADRATIC ansatz could work.\n# ψ = a*t - b*(x + x0)^2 \n# This was the first family suggested in the prompt.\n#