{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_bdrk_01KPPpToBH28CdGahk2CL2b4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01UHhhjyWxLWwKWPWKfLc3V1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"source /home/cyanz/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate wave && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport sympy as sp\nimport numpy as np\n\n# The characteristic ansatz ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(M+x-ct) has sq_psi = 0.\n# This means many L2 terms simplify drastically.\n# With sq_psi = 0 and alpha = 1:\n# L2 = -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde_sq)\n#      - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*tilde_sq^2\n#      + s^3*lam^3*(d_t(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_t) + c^2*d_x(phi^3*tilde_sq*psi_x))\n#\n# The (alpha-1) terms all vanish since alpha=1.\n# Same structure as the double-log, just with different expressions.\n#\n# The key structural problem remains: phi^3 grows without bound as x -> infinity.\n# UNLESS: phi^3 = exp(3*lam*psi) = exp(-3|lam|*(log(1+u)+log(M+v)))\n# = ((1+u)(M+v))^{-3|lam|} = ((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N}\n# For N = 20: phi^3 = ((1+u)(M+v))^{3/20}. Still grows!\n#\n# Actually wait. With lam = -1/N < 0:\n# phi = exp(lam*psi) = exp(-|lam|*(-log(1+u)-log(M+v))) = exp(|lam|*(log(1+u)+log(M+v)))\n# = ((1+u)(M+v))^{|lam|} = ((1+u)(M+v))^{1/N}\n# phi^3 = ((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N}\n# This still grows polynomially -> same L2 structural issue.\n#\n# FUNDAMENTAL INSIGHT: For ANY ansatz where phi -> infinity as x -> infinity \n# (which is REQUIRED by condition 2: lam*psi -> +infinity),\n# phi^3 grows even faster. The L2 s^3 terms involve phi^3, while the L2 s term\n# involves derivatives of phi*(...), which grow slower.\n# This STRUCTURAL MISMATCH appears to affect ALL ansatze satisfying condition 2.\n#\n# UNLESS: the s^3 terms in L2 have a special cancellation structure.\n#\n# Let me look more carefully at L2 for the characteristic ansatz.\n# With sq_psi = 0:\n# L2 = -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde)   [O(s)]\n#      - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*tilde^2        [O(s^3), this is >= 0 since -s^3 > 0]\n#      + s^3*lam^3*(div terms involving phi^3*tilde*grad_psi)  [O(s^3)]\n#\n# The second O(s^3) term is NON-NEGATIVE. The question is the third O(s^3) term.\n# div terms = d_t(phi^3*tilde*psi_t) + c^2*d_x(phi^3*tilde*psi_x)\n# \n# For the characteristic ansatz:\n# tilde = -4c^2/((1+u)(M+v))\n# psi_t = c*(-1/(1+u) + 1/(M+v))\n# psi_x = -1/(1+u) - 1/(M+v)\n# phi^3 = ((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N}\n#\n# phi^3*tilde = -4c^2*((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N}/((1+u)(M+v)) = -4c^2*((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N-1}\n# \n# phi^3*tilde*psi_t = -4c^3*((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N-1} * (-1/(1+u)+1/(M+v))\n# = -4c^3*((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N-1} * (-(M+v)+(1+u))/((1+u)(M+v))\n# = -4c^3*((1+u)(M+v))^{3/N-2} * ((1+u)-(M+v))\n# = -4c^3*(P^{3/N-2}) * (u-v+1-M) where P = (1+u)(M+v)\n#\n# u-v = 2ct, so (1+u)-(M+v) = 1+x+ct-(M+x-ct) = 1-M+2ct\n#\n# So phi^3*tilde*psi_t = -4c^3*P^{3/N-2}*(1-M+2ct)\n# d_t of this = d_t[-4c^3*P^{3/N-2}*(1-M+2ct)]\n# = -4c^3*[(3/N-2)*P^{3/N-3}*dP/dt*(1-M+2ct) + P^{3/N-2}*2c]\n# dP/dt = d_t[(1+u)(M+v)] = c*(M+v) + (1+u)*(-c) = c*(M+v-1-u) = c*(M-1-2ct)\n# Hmm, this is c*(M-1-u+v) = c*(M-1-(1+u)+(M+v)-M+1) ... let me just compute directly.\n\n# ALTERNATIVE STRATEGY: Maybe I should try the Run approach with a T-dependent M.\n# The verify engine doesn't have T as a symbol, BUT:\n# The problem says for GIVEN T, find parameters. The engine tests on a fixed grid.\n# If I define M in terms of T... but T isn't available.\n#\n# Actually, what if I just use M as a parameter (like x0) and set it to some expression?\n# The engine has x0 as an available symbol. I could redefine:\n# M = x0 and set x0 = 2*c + 1 in subs_dict.\n# For T in (0, 2], x-ct+2c = x+c(2-t) > 0 for x >= 0, t <= 2. ✓\n# But the problem requires arbitrary T. So M must be at least c*T.\n# Without T as a symbol, I can't express this.\n#\n# Let me check: is the verify engine tested with specific T values, or does it just\n# use t as unbounded?\n\n# Looking at the numerical check: x_range=(0, 50, 20), t_range=(0, 10, 20)\n# t goes up to 10. So if M = 11*c + 1, it would be safe for t <= 10.\n# But the symbolic check doesn't use any specific T.\n\n# KEY REALIZATION: The problem says \"for given arbitrary c > 0 and T > 0, find...\"\n# This means: for EACH (c, T), we produce (ψ, s, α, λ). \n# These can all depend on c and T. \n# But the verify engine tests symbolically (with c as a symbol and t as a free variable).\n# If the engine doesn't bound t, then t can be arbitrarily large in the symbolic check.\n# The characteristic ansatz ψ = -log(1+x+ct) - log(M+x-ct) requires M > cT for validity.\n# With M = c*T + 1, the function is well-defined on [0,∞) × [0,T].\n# But without T in the engine, we can't substitute this.\n\n# Actually, let me re-examine: does the verify engine's numerical check sample t?\n# Yes, t_range=(0, 10, 20). So t goes from 0 to 10.\n# And T doesn't appear in the symbolic expressions.\n# If I set M = 11*c + 1, the function is valid for t <= 10.\n# The symbolic checker might find issues beyond t = 10, but the numerical checker won't.\n\n# NEW PLAN: Forget about the characteristic ansatz for now.\n# Go back to the DOUBLE-LOG ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) which satisfies \n# conditions 1, 2, and sufficient.\n# The ONLY failure is L2 > 0.\n# \n# From the analysis above, L2 has the form:\n# L2/phi^3 = s * f(x,t,c) - s^3 * g(x,t,c)\n# where f > 0 (proven) and g can be positive or negative.\n# When g > 0: both terms have the same sign (since s < 0: s*f < 0, -s^3*g = |s|^3*g > 0)\n# Wait, I need to track signs more carefully.\n# L2/phi^3 = (const/phi^3) * [s * A_pos - s^3 * (B_mix)]\n# With s = -|s|:\n# = const/phi^3 * [-|s|*A_pos + |s|^3*B_mix]\n# For L2 > 0 (with the overall sign), we need... it depends on the overall sign.\n#\n# OK, the detailed numerics showed that with s = -1/100 (fixed constant), L2 is positive\n# on the engine's sampling grid (x up to 50, t up to 10, c in {0.5, 1, 2}).\n# The failure only appears at VERY large x (> 10^24) or very large c (> 10^53).\n#\n# IDEA: Can we define a candidate where the engine reports L2 > 0?\n# The engine's symbolic check likely returns \"unknown\" (not \"False\").\n# The numerical check uses a bounded grid.\n# If L2 is positive on the bounded grid, the engine reports \"likely_true\".\n# The final verdict depends on the verification report's analysis.\n#\n# But the REAL mathematical truth is that L2 < 0 at sufficiently large x.\n# The verify engine discovered this in previous rounds through careful analysis.\n#\n# I need to find a genuinely new approach that makes L2 > 0 for ALL x.\n\n# FINAL IDEA: What if the s^3 terms in L2 are IDENTICALLY ZERO?\n# This happens when tilde_sq(ψ) ≡ 0 AND sq(ψ) ≡ 0.\n# But then L2 = 0 (not > 0). Need L2 > 0 strictly.\n#\n# What if tilde_sq(ψ) is not identically zero but is VERY SMALL \n# in a carefully controlled way? For example, if tilde_sq(ψ) ~ epsilon * something,\n# then the s^3 terms scale as epsilon^2 (from tilde^2) while the s term \n# scales as epsilon (from sq(phi*tilde) involving tilde linearly).\n# So L2 ~ |s|*epsilon * positive - |s|^3 * epsilon^2 * ... ~ epsilon * |s| * (positive - |s|^2*epsilon*...)\n# For small enough epsilon: L2 > 0 regardless of |s|!\n#\n# How to make tilde_sq small but nonzero?\n# tilde_sq(ψ) = ψ_t^2 - c^2*ψ_x^2\n# For the double-log: = 1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(1+x)^2\n# This is NOT small in general.\n#\n# But what if ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) + epsilon*h(x,t) where h is chosen to make\n# tilde_sq nearly zero?\n# tilde_sq(ψ) ≈ tilde_sq_0 + epsilon*(2ψ_t*h_t - 2c^2*ψ_x*h_x) = 0\n# i.e., ψ_t*h_t - c^2*ψ_x*h_x = -tilde_sq_0/(2*epsilon)\n# This requires h to solve a PDE. Too complex.\n#\n# Actually, what about SCALING in time? Instead of -log(1+t), use -log(1+t/K)\n# for large K? Then ψ_t = -1/(K+t)/K, ψ_t^2 = 1/(K+t)^2\n# tilde_sq = 1/(K+t)^2 - c^2/(1+x)^2\n# For K >> c(1+x): tilde_sq ≈ 1/K^2 > 0, very small!\n# And the s^3 terms involving tilde^2 would be ~ 1/K^4, which is negligible.\n# The s term involving sq(phi*tilde) would scale differently.\n#\n# BUT: condition 2 requires lam*ψ -> +inf as x -> inf.\n# lam*ψ = |lam|*log(1+t/K) + |lam|*log(1+x). The log(1+x) part -> +inf. ✓\n# Condition 1 structure also changes since ψ_tt = 1/(K+t)^2, ψ_tt+c^2*ψ_xx = 1/(K+t)^2+c^2/(1+x)^2\n# Still works similarly.\n#\n# Actually, an even simpler approach: \n# ψ = -log(1+x)  (NO time dependence!)\n# Then ψ_t = 0, ψ_tt = 0\n# tilde_sq = 0 - c^2*ψ_x^2 = -c^2/(1+x)^2\n# sq_psi = 0 - c^2*ψ_xx = -c^2*(-1/(1+x)^2) = c^2/(1+x)^2\n# Hmm, wait: ψ_xx = d/dx(-1/(1+x)) = 1/(1+x)^2\n# sq_psi = ψ_tt - c^2*ψ_xx = 0 - c^2/(1+x)^2\n# tilde_sq = 0 - c^2/(1+x)^2 = -c^2/(1+x)^2\n# So sq_psi = tilde_sq = -c^2/(1+x)^2. Both are the SAME and NEGATIVE.\n#\n# L with alpha=1: L = lam^2*phi*tilde - 0 = lam^2*phi*(-c^2/(1+x)^2) < 0\n# Actually wait: (alpha-1)=0, so L = lam^2*phi*tilde_sq\n# L1 = lam^2*phi*(0 + c^2/(1+x)^2) + lam*phi*(0 + c^2/(1+x)^2)\n#    = (lam^2+lam)*phi*c^2/(1+x)^2 = lam(lam+1)*phi*c^2/(1+x)^2\n# With lam=-1/N: lam(lam+1) = -(N-1)/N^2 < 0. So L1 < 0. ✓\n#\n# Condition 1: L1 ≤ L ≤ -L1\n# L = lam^2*phi*(-c^2/(1+x)^2) = -phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2)\n# L is negative.\n# -L1 = (N-1)/N^2 * phi * c^2/(1+x)^2\n# Need L >= L1: L - L1 = -phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2) + (N-1)/N^2*phi*c^2/(1+x)^2 \n# = phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2) * (-1 + N-1) = phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2) * (N-2)\n# For N >= 3: L - L1 = positive. So L > L1 iff L - L1 > 0 iff N > 2. ✓\n# Need L <= -L1: L + L1 = -phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2) - (N-1)/N^2*phi*c^2/(1+x)^2\n# = -phi*c^2/(N^2*(1+x)^2) * N = -phi*c^2/(N*(1+x)^2) < 0\n# So L < -L1 always. ✓\n#\n# Condition 2: lam*ψ = -lam*log(1+x) = |lam|*log(1+x) -> +inf as x -> inf. ✓\n#\n# Sufficient condition: L1^2 - L^2 >= c^2*(phi_xt)^2\n# phi = exp(lam*ψ) = (1+x)^{-lam} = (1+x)^{1/N}\n# phi_t = 0 (no t dependence!)\n# phi_xt = 0!\n# So c^2*(phi_xt)^2 = 0.\n# L1^2 - L^2 >= 0. From above: L1 = -(N-1)/N^2 * phi*c^2/(1+x)^2, L = -1/N^2*phi*c^2/(1+x)^2\n# L1^2 - L^2 = (phi*c^2/(1+x)^2)^2 / N^4 * ((N-1)^2 - 1) = ... >= 0 for N >= 2. ✓\n#\n# L2 with ψ = -log(1+x):\n# sq_psi = tilde_sq = -c^2/(1+x)^2\n# With alpha=1:\n# L2 = -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde) - s^3*lam^4*phi^3*tilde^2 + s^3*lam^3*div_terms\n# \n# tilde = -c^2/(1+x)^2\n# phi*tilde = -(1+x)^{1/N}*c^2/(1+x)^2 = -c^2*(1+x)^{1/N-2}\n# sq(phi*tilde) = d_tt(phi*tilde) - c^2*d_xx(phi*tilde)\n# d_tt(phi*tilde) = 0 (no t dependence!)\n# d_xx(phi*tilde) = d_xx(-c^2*(1+x)^{1/N-2})\n# = -c^2*(1/N-2)(1/N-3)*(1+x)^{1/N-4}\n# sq(phi*tilde) = 0 - c^2*(-c^2*(1/N-2)(1/N-3)*(1+x)^{1/N-4})\n# = c^4*(1/N-2)(1/N-3)*(1+x)^{1/N-4}\n# For N=20: (1/20-2)(1/20-3) = (-39/20)(-59/20) = 39*59/400 = 2301/400 > 0\n# So sq(phi*tilde) > 0! ✓\n#\n# Term_A = -(1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde) = (|s|/2)*(1/N^2)*c^4*2301/400*(1+x)^{1/N-4} > 0\n#\n# Now the s^3 terms. phi^3 = (1+x)^{3/N}. tilde^2 = c^4/(1+x)^4.\n# Term_B = -s^3*lam^4*phi^3*tilde^2 = |s|^3*(1/N^4)*(1+x)^{3/N}*c^4/(1+x)^4\n# = |s|^3*c^4/(N^4*(1+x)^{4-3/N})\n#\n# div_terms: d_t(phi^3*tilde*psi_t) = d_t(0) = 0 (since psi_t = 0!)\n# c^2*d_x(phi^3*tilde*psi_x): \n# phi^3*tilde*psi_x = (1+x)^{3/N}*(-c^2/(1+x)^2)*(-1/(1+x))\n# = c^2*(1+x)^{3/N-3}\n# d_x of this = c^2*(3/N-3)*(1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# c^2*d_x(...) = c^4*(3/N-3)*(1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# For N=20: 3/20-3 = -57/20 < 0. So this is NEGATIVE.\n# Term_C = s^3*lam^3*c^4*(3/N-3)*(1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# = -|s|^3*(-1/N^3)*c^4*(3/N-3)*(1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# = |s|^3/(N^3)*c^4*(3/N-3)*(1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# Since 3/N-3 < 0: Term_C < 0. This is the problematic negative contribution.\n#\n# L2 = Term_A + Term_B + Term_C\n# = c^4*(1+x)^{3/N-4} * [|s|*K_A*(1+x)^{1/N-3/N} + |s|^3*K_B + |s|^3*K_C]\n# Wait, let me align the powers:\n# Term_A has (1+x)^{1/N-4}, Term_B has (1+x)^{3/N-4}, Term_C has (1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# Factor (1+x)^{3/N-4}:\n# Term_A = |s|*c^4*2301/(800*N^2) * (1+x)^{1/N-4} = |s|*c^4*2301/(800*N^2) * (1+x)^{3/N-4}*(1+x)^{1/N-3/N}\n# Hmm, 1/N - 3/N = -2/N. So (1+x)^{1/N-4} = (1+x)^{3/N-4}*(1+x)^{-2/N}\n# So Term_A/[(1+x)^{3/N-4}] = |s|*c^4*2301/(800*N^2)*(1+x)^{-2/N}\n# Term_B/[(1+x)^{3/N-4}] = |s|^3*c^4/N^4\n# Term_C/[(1+x)^{3/N-4}] = |s|^3*c^4*(3/N-3)/N^3\n#\n# As x -> infinity: (1+x)^{-2/N} -> 0, while the s^3 terms are constant.\n# So for large enough x, Term_B + Term_C dominates.\n# Term_B + Term_C = |s|^3*c^4*(1+x)^{3/N-4} * (1/N^4 + (3/N-3)/N^3)\n# = |s|^3*c^4*(1+x)^{3/N-4} * (1/N^4 + 3/N^4 - 3/N^3)\n# = |s|^3*c^4*(1+x)^{3/N-4} * (4/N^4 - 3/N^3)\n# = |s|^3*c^4*(1+x)^{3/N-4}/N^4 * (4 - 3N)\n# For N = 20: 4 - 60 = -56 < 0.\n# So Term_B + Term_C < 0 for large x! (since (1+x)^{3/N-4} > 0)\n# And Term_A -> 0 as x -> infinity.\n# So L2 < 0 for large x. Same structural problem!\n#\n# But wait: (1+x)^{3/N-4} DECAYS as x -> infinity (since 3/N-4 < 0 for N >= 1).\n# So Term_B + Term_C -> 0 as well!\n# The question is: which goes to 0 faster, Term_A or (Term_B + Term_C)?\n# Term_A ~ (1+x)^{3/N-4-2/N} = (1+x)^{1/N-4}\n# Term_B+C ~ (1+x)^{3/N-4}\n# Ratio: Term_A / (Term_B+C) ~ (1+x)^{-2/N} -> 0\n# So Terms_B+C (which is negative) decays slower -> L2 < 0 for large x.\n#\n# ARGH. Same structural issue. The s^3 terms always dominate at large x.\n# \n# The ONLY way to fix this seems to be:\n# 1. Make |s| depend on x (not allowed - s is a constant for each (c,T) pair)\n# 2. Make the s^3 coefficient have a different sign (positive instead of negative)\n# 3. Make tilde_sq = 0 and alpha != 1 to get a non-degenerate L2\n#\n# Let me try option 3 more carefully.\n# With tilde_sq(ψ) = 0: ALL the s^3 terms vanish (they all contain tilde_sq).\n# L2 = (1/2)*(alpha-1)*s*lam*sq(phi*sq_psi) - (1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde_sq)\n# = (1/2)*(alpha-1)*s*lam*sq(phi*sq_psi) - 0  [since tilde_sq = 0 inside the sq operator too]\n# Wait: sq(phi*tilde_sq) = sq(phi*0) = 0 only if tilde_sq ≡ 0. ✓\n# So L2 = (1/2)*(alpha-1)*s*lam*sq(phi*sq_psi)\n# For L2 > 0: need (alpha-1)*s*lam*sq(phi*sq_psi) > 0\n# With s < 0, lam < 0: s*lam > 0. So need (alpha-1)*sq(phi*sq_psi) > 0.\n#\n# If we also have sq_psi = 0: then sq(phi*0) = 0, so L2 = 0. Not > 0.\n# So we need tilde_sq = 0 but sq_psi ≠ 0. \n# tilde_sq(ψ) = ψ_t^2 - c^2*ψ_x^2 = 0 means |ψ_t| = c|ψ_x|.\n# sq_psi = ψ_tt - c^2*ψ_xx ≠ 0.\n# \n# Example: ψ = f(x+ct) (forward characteristic) has sq_psi = 0 AND tilde_sq = 0.\n# Both zero - not useful.\n# But ψ = f(x+ct) + g(x) (add a purely spatial term):\n# ψ_t = c*f', ψ_x = f' + g'\n# tilde_sq = c^2*(f')^2 - c^2*(f'+g')^2 = c^2*(f'^2 - f'^2 - 2f'g' - g'^2) = -c^2*(2f'g'+g'^2)\n# This is not zero (unless g = 0). So pure characteristic + spatial is not eikonal.\n#\n# WHAT IF: ψ_t = c*ψ_x exactly?\n# Then ψ = f(x+ct) for some f. sq_psi = 0, tilde_sq = 0. Back to L2 = 0.\n# ψ_t = -c*ψ_x: same thing.\n#\n# Can we have |ψ_t| = c|ψ_x| pointwise but ψ NOT be a function of a single characteristic?\n# No - if ψ_t = c*ψ_x everywhere, then ψ is constant along backward characteristics,\n# so ψ = f(x+ct). If ψ_t = -c*ψ_x: ψ = g(x-ct).\n# If the sign alternates: not smooth. Not allowed.\n#\n# So tilde_sq = 0 implies L2 = 0 or all s^3 terms vanish but L2 might still be 0.\n# This approach doesn't yield L2 > 0.\n\nprint(\"CONCLUSION: Need to find a ψ where L2 > 0 for all x in [0,inf).\")\nprint(\"The log-based ansatze all have the structural problem that s^3 terms\")\nprint(\"involve phi^3 which grows faster than the s terms at large x.\")\nprint()\nprint(\"NEW APPROACH: What if we use alpha < 1?\")\nprint(\"With alpha < 1, the (alpha-1)*s*lam term in L2_O(s) gives a NEGATIVE contribution\")\nprint(\"(since (alpha-1) < 0, s*lam > 0). But this also changes the L2_s^3 terms.\")\nprint(\"Specifically, the s^3*(alpha-1)*lam^3*phi^3*tilde*sq term (which was 0 for alpha=1)\")\nprint(\"now contributes. For the double-log (tilde = sq): this is s^3*(alpha-1)*lam^3*phi^3*sq^2\")\nprint(\"With s^3 < 0, (alpha-1) < 0, lam^3 < 0: product is (-)(-)(-) = (-) < 0.\")\nprint(\"So this adds ANOTHER negative s^3 term. Worse!\")\nprint()\nprint(\"What about alpha > 1 (but satisfying condition 1)?\")\nprint(\"From earlier: need |alpha-1+|lam|| <= |lam+1| for condition 1.\")\nprint(\"alpha-1+1/N <= (N-1)/N => alpha <= 1 - 2/N = 18/20 = 9/10 for N=20. Must be < 1!\")\nprint(\"So alpha MUST be <= 9/10 for condition 1 to hold with N=20 and the double-log.\")\nprint(\"In fact, alpha in [0, 1-2/N] union [1, 1] = [0, 1-2/N] or alpha=1.\")\nprint(\"(At alpha=1, |lam-alpha+1| = |lam| = 1/N < (N-1)/N. Strictlyholds.)\")\nprint(\"Actually, need |lam-(alpha-1)| <= |1+lam|.\")\nprint(\"|(-1/N)-(alpha-1)| = |1-alpha-1/N| <= (N-1)/N\")\nprint(\"Case alpha < 1: 1-alpha-1/N <= (N-1)/N => 1-alpha <= 1 => alpha >= 0\")\nprint(\"And 1-alpha-1/N >= 0 requires alpha <= 1-1/N = 19/20\")\nprint(\"If 1-alpha-1/N < 0 (alpha > 19/20 and alpha < 1): |1-alpha-1/N| = alpha-1+1/N\")\nprint(\"Need alpha-1+1/N <= (N-1)/N => alpha <= 1-2/N = 9/10. Contradiction with alpha > 19/20!\")\nprint(\"So for alpha in (19/20, 1): condition 1 fails!\")\nprint(\"alpha must be in [0, 19/20] or alpha = 1.\")\nprint()\nprint(\"With alpha = 0 (try something extreme):\")\nprint(\"L = lam*(lam+1)*phi*sq_psi = (-1/20)(19/20)*phi*sq_psi = -(19/400)*phi*sq_psi\")\nprint(\"L1 = -(19/400)*phi*P  [same as before]\")\nprint(\"|L| = (19/400)*phi*|sq_psi|\")\nprint(\"-L1 = (19/400)*phi*P\")\nprint(\"|L| <= -L1 iff |sq_psi| <= P. Always true. ✓\")\nprint()\nprint(\"BUT for alpha=0: L2_O(s) = (1/2)*(-1)*s*lam*sq(phi*sq) - (1/2)*s*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde)\")\nprint(\"For double-log (sq = tilde): = (1/2)*s*lam*(-1-lam)*sq(phi*tilde)\")\nprint(\"= (1/2)*s*lam*(-1+1/N)*sq(phi*tilde) [with lam=-1/N]\")\nprint(\"= (1/2)*|s|*|lam|*(1-1/N)*sq(phi*tilde)\")\nprint(\"Compared to alpha=1: (1/2)*|s|*lam^2*sq(phi*tilde) = (1/2)*|s|/N^2*sq(phi*tilde)\")\nprint(\"Ratio: alpha=0 gives (1-1/N)/N, alpha=1 gives 1/N^2. Ratio: N(1-1/N)/1 = N-1.\")\nprint(\"So alpha=0 gives (N-1) TIMES LARGER O(s) coefficient!\")\nprint(\"For N=20: 19 times larger! This could be the key to fixing L2!\")\n\nPYEOF\n","description":"Explore alpha=0 strategy for L2","timeout":120000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":134,"cache_read_input_tokens":151978,"cache_creation":{"epheme